{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "The Acquisition System and Its Components", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 4, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 1, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "A2.1", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "These are words you use to describe data acquisition systems.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Describe common activities at work to a visitor using short sentences in familiar spoken and written situations.  Understand information about data and its structure in simple spoken and written situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Identifies the expressions and vocabulary necessary for the description of places of work and jobs in common written and spoken situations.   Describes common activities related to Software Programming through the use of appropriate expressions, grammar and vocabulary in written or spoken situations according to the abilities related to this level.", "type": "secondary_1"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "enpe7n3le01ob04re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Countable and Uncountable Nouns with Many and Much", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We use <b>much</b> and <b>many</b> to refer to a considerable quantity of a noun.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "There are some nouns in software acquisition systems that we can quantify with <span class=\"strong\">much</span> and <span class=\"strong\">many</span> to express a great amount of something. <br/><br/>  We use <span class=\"strong\">much</span> to talk about a huge amount of an uncountable noun. <br/><br/>  Example: <br/> <span class=\"italic\"> <span class=\"strong\">Much</span> information<br/> <span class=\"strong\">Much</span> speed</span><br/><br/>  We use <span class=\"strong\">many</span> to talk about a big number of countable nouns. <br/><br/>  Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\"> <span class=\"strong\">Many</span> datum<br/> <span class=\"strong\">Many</span> signals</span><br/><br/>  Let's take a look at some other examples of <span class=\"strong\">countable</span> and <span class=\"strong\">uncountable</span> nous that we can use with <span class=\"strong\">many</span> and <span class=\"strong\">much</span>: ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "{\"rows\":4,\"columns\":2,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"><span class=\\\"strong\\\">Countable Nouns</span></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<div class=\\\"center_text\\\"><span class=\\\"strong\\\">Uncountable Nouns</span></div>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Many</span> devices\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Much</span> information\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Many</span> transducers\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Much</span> resolution\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Many</span> instruments\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<span class=\\\"strong\\\">Much</span> ratio\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\" intro_table_border instruction_table_background\"}", "type": "table"}, {"content": "A data acquisition system may have many components.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "If the device measures a signal many times, the sound is better.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "We sometimes need much resolution to display data clearly.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Simple Present Tense and Present Continuous in Questions", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "We can ask questions in <span class=\"strong\">simple preset</span> by using the auxiliary verb <span class=\"strong\">do</span> or <span class=\"strong\">does</span>. On the other hand, we use the verb <span class=\"strong\">to be</span> as an auxiliary to create questions in <span class=\"strong\">present continuous</span>. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "We usually make questions in English by setting the <span class=\"strong\">auxiliary verb</span> before the subject. <br/><br/>  Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\"><span class=\"strong\">Do</span> you need a new system? <br/> <span class=\"strong\">Are</span> you sorting the requirements now? </span><br/><br/>  The auxiliary verb of the simple present is <span class=\"strong\">do</span> which becomes <span class=\"strong\">does</span> in third person (<span class=\"italic\">he, she</span>, and <span class=\"italic\">it</span>). <br/><br/>  Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\"> <span class=\"strong\">Does</span> he know about data acquisition? <br/> <span class=\"strong\">Do</span> computers interpret data? </span><br/><br/>  In <span class=\"strong\">present continuous</span>, the auxiliary verb is the verb <span class=\"strong\">be</span>. <br/><br/>  Examples: <br/> <span class=\"italic\"> <span class=\"strong\">Is</span> the software displaying the data? <br/> <span class=\"strong\">Are</span> we acquiring any data? </span> <br/><br/>  In the examples above, the first word is the auxiliary verb; when that verb is at the beginning of the question, we are looking for a <span class=\"strong\">yes/no answer</span>, but we can also use <span class=\"strong\">WH questions</span> to ask for information. <br/> <br/>    Examples: <br/>  <span class=\"italic\"> <span class=\"strong\">Where</span> do you work? <br/> <span class=\"strong\">What type of data</span> are you collecting? </span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "What are you doing?", "type": "example"}, {"content": "Why are you asking me that?", "type": "example"}, {"co